A tiny decision-scoring function that weighs serving engines against a few criteria, the way you might quantify the trade-offs described above:

```python
# A tiny illustrative engine-scoring function (NOT production code).

engines = {
    "vLLM": {"portability": 9, "raw_speed_nvidia": 7, "ease_of_use": 8, "feature_breadth": 8},
    "TensorRT-LLM": {"portability": 3, "raw_speed_nvidia": 10, "ease_of_use": 5, "feature_breadth": 6},
    "TGI": {"portability": 8, "raw_speed_nvidia": 7, "ease_of_use": 7, "feature_breadth": 7},
}


def score_engine(metrics, weights):
    return sum(metrics[criterion] * weight for criterion, weight in weights.items())


def rank_engines(engines, weights):
    scored = {name: score_engine(metrics, weights) for name, metrics in engines.items()}
    return sorted(scored.items(), key=lambda pair: pair[1], reverse=True)


# Scenario A: committed to NVIDIA hardware, latency-critical, engineering time available.
nvidia_latency_weights = {
    "portability": 0.1, "raw_speed_nvidia": 0.6, "ease_of_use": 0.1, "feature_breadth": 0.2,
}

# Scenario B: mixed hardware fleet, want to move fast with limited engineering time.
portable_easy_weights = {
    "portability": 0.4, "raw_speed_nvidia": 0.1, "ease_of_use": 0.4, "feature_breadth": 0.1,
}

print(rank_engines(engines, nvidia_latency_weights))
print(rank_engines(engines, portable_easy_weights))
```

Notice that Scenario A (heavy weight on NVIDIA raw speed) ranks TensorRT-LLM first, while Scenario B (heavy weight on portability and ease of use) ranks it last — the same requirements-first decision the lesson describes, just made concrete as weighted scores instead of an abstract heuristic.
